Z||000000||mrgone||06-06-2001||03:54 AM||||oopsie. i looked under the rear of my car with the trunk open today. <P>"whats this??? sunlight shining through??"<P>lift up the carpet, sure enough, the top sidewalls of my trunk each have a hole about the size of a large brick<P>i like to stand in the trunk when i work on the engine too.... hehe<P>now... <BR>1) do i have to remove the corrosion down to solid metal or can i glass over the rust.<P>2) can i just use liquid fiberglass and fiber cloth, or do i have to reinforce the opening with steel mesh or somthing<P>3) do i have to roughen the material im going to be sticking the fiber mesh to? like with sandpaper or will it just stick?<P>4) installation of fiberglass: inside or outside first? work top to bottom?<P>5) anything i should know? (flammability, toxic fumes, glue hand to face etc....)||||reg||12
Z||000001||Indiana_resto_guy||06-06-2001||07:00 AM||||#1) yes you do and no, you can't<BR>#2) You may do both, I usually do just the first. Since the area of repair is mostly flat, I first up make a flat piece and let it set then cut it to size and glass it in from both sides.<BR>#3) Yes, get a good overlap of bare metal of at least 1.5 to 2 inches (3.75 to 5 mm).<BR>#4) see #2.<BR>#5) Read the label (yes to all fears)||||reg||1
Z||000002||Alex4mula||06-06-2001||11:17 AM||||There's a product called POR-15 which you may want to use in your repair. They also sell a kit with a fiber-mat for that kind of repairs. Very good products. They anounce in HotRod and CarCrafts magazines. Also search the web for their URL.<P>------------------<BR>Alex4mula<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.zing.com/picture/p69fbfe3e99ec5a0b81c10afcc578ff95/fe6d5c42.jpg.orig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
